  "audioFile": "audio/demo_voice.mp3",
  "text": "At twenty percent, you open the camera... and your phone dies. Let it rest or warm up, and it can turn back on. The battery was never empty. The percentage is only an estimate from a tiny chip. Think of your battery as a water tank. The water is charge. The pressure is voltage. Heavy apps pull hard, and cold or aging narrows the pipe, so the pressure drops. Below a minimum, your phone shuts off. University of Michigan researchers watched a phone die at minus fifteen degrees Celsius, still showing thirty percent. Your phone didn't run out of energy. It ran out of pressure. And that's why...",
